No, I think they are ordinary individuals:

    lo cimei be lo broda
=3D  zo'e noi ke'a cimei lo broda
   "things, which are three and among the broda

>> ko'a (n+1)mei .ijo su'oi da poi pamei zo'u su'oi de poi (n)mei zo'u ge
>> da na me de gi ro'oi di poi ke'a me ko'a zo'u ga di me da gi di me de
>> "A are n+1 iff there's some X which is one and some Y which are n such
>> that X is not among Y and for every Z among A, Z is X or Z are among
>> Y"
>
> [Not quite enough, since =A0for every W among Y, WX is among A but not am=
ong Z nor is it X.]

Right, I complicated myself unnecessarily. I should have defined the
"su'o(n)mei" first, and only then the "(n)mei".

"su'o(pa)mei" is true of anything.

"su'o(n+1)mei":

A are at least n+1 iff there's X among A and Y among A such that X are
at least n and Y are not among X

Then "su'e(n)mei":

A are at most n iff A are not at least n+1

And finally:

A are n iff A are at least n and at most n

mu'o mi'e xorxes

